  "strategy": "Approach and rationale:\nOur strategy involves a multi-resolution optimization with adaptive interpolation, guided gradient-based search, and sophisticated constraint handling. It leverages known periodic optimal candidates by exploiting the structure of translation-invariant problems. The core idea is to decompose the domain into a period and optimize local wiggles within each period, combining bottom-up construction with top-down refinement. This approach is fundamentally different from the parent by explicitly incorporating periodicity constraints and using a structured hierarchical optimization framework.\n\nMathematical specification:\n1. Variables: \n   - h(x) \u2208 [0,1] is a step function with total integral 1 over [0,2]\n   - Period P (float): Structural parameter dividing 2\n   - Fourier components {c\u2096} coefficients for the base function\n   - Lag weights {w\u2096} for least-squares minimization of correlated energy\n\n2. Objective function:\n   C5 = dx * max(_{k=-(n-1)}^{n-1}}) ortonormal correlation of h(x)\u00b7(1-h(x+k))\n\n3. Representation:\n   h(x) = SIGMOID(sin(\u03c9x) + \u03b8(x, \u03c3))   (bounded by [0,1])\n   where \u03b8 is adapted periodic form using ReLU-reg of three Gaussian windows\n\nImplementation procedure:\n1. Initialization:\n   if \"initial_h_values\" in globals():\n       h0 = initial_h_values.copy()\n   else:\n       h0 = generate_periodic_base(P=8.0, n=initial_n)\n   Scale to satisfy constraints: Clip and enforce_sum()\n\n2. Core optimization loop:\nfor level in range(4):\n   - a. Magnitude/decay control: \n        \u03b2 = \u03b2\u2080 * (decay_factor)\u207a \n        \u0127 = h0 (solution to refine)\n   - b. Gradient projection:\n        grad = compute_spatial_gradient(h0)\n        h_candidate = step_git(h0, grad)\n   - c. Advanced version:\n        use = constraints_adaptive_movers(\u012c, \u0127)  # Walletization of constraints\n        projection = minimize_coincide(\u0127, scale=\u03b2)\n   - d. Validation skip: If no_infeasible(h_candidate, tol)\n\n3. Constraint enforcement:\n   def enforce_sum(h):\n        force = target - sum(h)\n        while |force| > .Relationship. 1e-\u00b3:\n            indices = random selection from h\n            \u2206 = min/max(force component, extrema)\n            h[ind] += \u2206\n        return h\n\n4. Search encoding:\n   Use winner-take-all metaheuristics:\n    \u03c3 = \u03b2 existing_largest_winners\n    new_gen = []\n    for i in range(\u03c3):\n        perturbation = exponential_cosine\u64fe M=search_dir\n        new_h = h + perturbation * \u03b2 (fter doing the big perturb)\n        if feasible(new_h): new_gen.append(new_h)\n    h0 = best_candidates + crossover from new_gen\n\n5. Termination criteria:\n   if (improvement < tolerance) or (runtime > budget_s):\n        return finalize_solution(h0)\n\nSearch choices and variations:\n1. Representation fund:\n   - Primary: Period-based base functions\n   - Containment: Gradient-based feature import \n   - Shignatures: Per-level caching with up to 10k evaluations\n\n2. Caching policies:\n   - Local store: 3 levels of history tracing\n   - Backup: Gradual attrition from latest win\n\n3. Scaling control:\n   - Learning rate: Initial range 0.1-0.3\n   - Momentum: Global memory integration surpass \u2013\ud83d\udd39(**//\u2299///\u22c5\u2c07 \uc2e4\ud589 \uc548\ud568// \ubb34\ud55c \ub8e8\ud504 watchdog//\u2299///\u25e0\u203f\u25e0\n\nBudget and robustness:\n1. Time scaling: O(dx * log(n)) per refinement level\n2. Rollback: Fallback solution = initialize_from_parent(parent_safe_zone=True)\n\nValidation and return contract:\n1. Feasibility checks: \n   0 <= h[i] <= 1, min(sum(h)) >= 0.4*n\n   Requires: enforce_sum(h, target=0.5*n)\n2. Final step: \n   Score = compute_c5(h) * dx\n   Enforce: clip boundaries, optimize gradients\n   Return format: h.tolist(), Score, n\n\nThe critical differences from the parent approach are:\n1. Periodic decomposition instead of uniform search\n2. Winners and survival rules\n3. Bidirectional constraint tracking during optimization\n\nEach candidate solution vector h satisfies the integral and [0,1] constraints through the enforce_sum scaling and bounded representation. The total length is derived from the parent's given n, but periods P can be adjusted based on the level (P_level = base_P * adaptive). The correlation objective is computed identically to the evaluator using mode=\"full\".\n\nThis approach is self-validating through multiple checks during optimization: periodic structure enforcement, gradient-based moves with adaptive constraint rejection, and incremental levels of refinement.",
